<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter7_traversing-strings">
    <title>Traversing Strings</title>

    <p>
        To <term>traverse</term> a string means to visit its characters in
        sequence. Chapter 6 introduced several loop forms. Strings give us a
        useful setting for practicing all three of the main traversal patterns.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter7_indexed-string-traversal">
        <title>Indexed <c>for</c> Loops</title>

        <p>
            Use an indexed <c>for</c> loop when the program needs both a
            character and its position.
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_indexed_string_traversal"
                 language="cpp" interactive="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

int main() {
    std::string word{"C++"};

    for (std::size_t index = 0; index < word.size(); ++index) {
        std::cout << index << ": " << word[index] << '\n';
    }

    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>
		
	        <warning>
            <p>
			Avoid using standard signed <c>int</c> types when dealing with container indexing and size. 
			Instead, use <c>size_t</c> or, even better, use the C++17 <c>auto</c> keyword to let the 
			compiler automatically deduce the perfect, safe matching type for you.
            </p>
        </warning>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter7_range-string-traversal">
        <title>Range-Based <c>for</c> Loops</title>

        <p>
            Use a range-based <c>for</c> loop when the program needs each
            character but does not need its index. A reference lets the loop
            modify the original characters; a <c>const</c> reference avoids
            copying and prevents modification.
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_range_string_traversal"
                 language="cpp" interactive="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

int main() {
    std::string word{"C++"};

    for (const char character : word) {
        std::cout << character << '\n';
    }

    for (auto &character : word) {
        character = '_'; // 
    }
	std::cout << word << '\n';
    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter7_while-string-traversal">
        <title><c>while</c> Loops</title>

        <p>
            A <c>while</c> loop is useful when traversal is controlled by a
            condition that may be expressed directly. The index must be updated
            so that the loop eventually stops.
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_while_string_traversal"
                 language="cpp" interactive="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

int main() {
    std::string word{"banana"};
    std::size_t index = 0;

    while (index < word.size()) {
        std::cout << word[index] << '\n';
        ++index;
    }

    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter7_counting-string-characters">
        <title>Counting Characters</title>

        <p>
            A counter can record how often a character appears. The function
            below uses a <c>const</c> reference because it only reads the
            caller's string.
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_count_character"
                 language="cpp" interactive="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <cstddef>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

std::size_t count_character(const std::string& text, char target) {
    std::size_t count = 0;

    for (const char character : text) {
        if (character == target) {
            ++count;
        }
    }

    return count;
}

int main() {
    std::cout << count_character("banana", 'a') << '\n';
    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>
    </subsection>
</section>
